2. SIDEBAR REORDER
   Current order (index.html): Translate / Practice (Flashcards, Recall Mode) /
   Pronunciation (Pinyin Chart, Pinyin Guide) / Grammar (Guide, Flashcards) /
   Courses (14-Day) / Simplified-Traditional (Guide, Cheat Sheet, Flashcards).
   Intent: most-used items at the top so they're the shortest click.
   Open: decide the real usage order before moving anything. Candidate order is
   Translate -> Practice -> Courses -> Grammar -> Pronunciation -> S/T, on the
   theory that S/T is reference material consulted rarely, while Courses is daily.
   Do not reorder on a guess; confirm first.

3. EXTRACT SHARED JS AND CSS
   Problem: 9 pages duplicate speak() with the same TTS engine toggle, protocol
   routing, and fallback logic. A TTS bug fix must be applied to 9 files. CSS
   resets, dark-mode variables, and base typography are copy-pasted across every
   page's <style> block with minor drift.

   Constraints:
     - Must keep working on file:// (no fetch for local files, no ES modules
       without a server). Classic <script src="..."> and <link rel="stylesheet">
       with relative paths only.
     - No build step. No bundler.
     - sw.js STATIC_ASSETS must include the new shared files. Bump CACHE_NAME.

   Phase 1 - shared/tts.js (highest ROI):
     - Extract speak(), the engine toggle, and protocol-based routing into one
       file. Pages load it with <script src="shared/tts.js"></script> and call
       speak(text, lang) directly.
     - Migrate the 4 pages already using the external-TTS-primary pattern first
       (phrase_reference, grammar_guide, mandarin_in_14_days, mandarin_translation).
     - Then migrate the 5 legacy Web-Speech-API-only pages (flashcards,
       grammar_flashcards, pinyin_chart, simplified_traditional_flashcards,
       radicals_reference) to the shared module, switching them to the preferred
       pattern in the process.
     - After migration, each page's inline speak() is deleted entirely.

   Phase 2 - shared/base.css (lower priority):
     - Extract the common reset (box-sizing, margin, font stack), CSS custom
       properties (--bg, --text, --accent, --card-bg, etc.), and responsive
       breakpoints that are already near-identical across pages.
     - Keep page-specific layout and component styles inline. The shared file
       is ONLY the foundation that every page would otherwise copy.
     - Each page adds <link rel="stylesheet" href="shared/base.css"> before
       its own <style> block so page-specific rules win by cascade order.

   What NOT to extract:
     - Translation logic (only mandarin_translation.html uses it).
     - Page-specific JS (flashcard shuffle, quiz grading, handwriting canvas).
     - Component-level CSS (card styles, quiz buttons, sidebar). These vary
       enough per page that a shared file would become a grab-bag.


PRIORITY 4 — new content
------------------------

4. COURSE 2 — "HANDLING THE REPLY"   (was: "Next 14-Day Course")
   STATUS: AGREED. Course 2 is the same travel situations taught from the OTHER side
   of the conversation — what he HEARS back — not deeper vocabulary on the same topics.

   The failure it fixes, concretely:
     Him:    我要这个            ("I want this one")  <- Course 1 taught him this. Perfect.
     Waiter: 在这儿吃还是带走?    ("eat here or take away?")
     Him:    ...frozen.
   His Mandarin didn't fail. Course 1 taught him every sentence he SAYS and not one
   sentence he HEARS. Course 2 closes that.

   Context: Course 1 answers "can he survive 14 days in China with just this book?"
   It teaches him to SPEAK first — 200-odd phrases he initiates. The gap is that in
   a real exchange the other person answers, and nothing prepares him for that.
   His stated goal is exactly this: handle replies that weren't scripted.

   So Course 2 should NOT be "the same lessons but harder vocabulary". It should be
   the same situations from the OTHER side of the counter.

   Per topic (start with the highest-traffic three: Ordering Food, Shopping,
   Directions), each lesson covers:
     - The 5-10 replies he will actually hear, as audio-first drills. e.g. for food:
       几位? / 在这儿吃还是带走? / 要不要辣? / 还要别的吗? / 扫码点单
     - The 2-3 ways each reply gets phrased, because real people vary the wording.
     - His response options for each.
     - Repair phrases — the highest-value item in the whole course and currently
       absent everywhere:
         请再说一遍  (please say it again)
         说慢一点    (slower please)
         我听不懂    (I don't understand)
         这个用中文怎么说? (how do you say this in Chinese?)
       These are what convert a dead-end into a conversation. They should arguably
       be pulled forward into Course 1 as well.
     - A branching mini-dialogue: his line -> one of 2-3 possible replies -> his
       next line. Even a simple scripted branch beats a linear dialogue for teaching
       him that replies vary.
   Format: audio-first. He should hear the reply before seeing it.


